General elections were held in [[Sierra Leone]] on 7 March 2018 to elect the President, Parliament and local councils.<ref name="thesierraleonetelegraph.com">[http://www.thesierraleonetelegraph.com/?p=15039 President Koroma announces election date – March 7th 2018] The Sierra Leone Telegraph, 15 February 2017</ref> Incumbent President [[Ernest Bai Koroma]] did not run for another term, as he was constitutionally ineligible, having served the maximum ten years in office.

No presidential candidate received the 55% of the vote required to win in the first round, meaning a second round of voting was held on 31 March between the top two candidates, opposition leader [[Julius Maada Bio]] of the [[Sierra Leone People's Party]] and [[Samura Kamara]] of the ruling [[All People's Congress]]; the two were separated by under 15,000 votes in the first round. Bio was subsequently elected with 51.8% of the vote.  International observers hailed the election as being "orderly, free and fair" despite the fact it was "hotly contested."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.dw.com/en/sierra-leone-heads-into-hotly-contested-election-runoff/a-42813380|title = Sierra Leone heads into hotly contested election runoff &#124; DW &#124; 30.03.2018| website=[[Deutsche Welle]] }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.thesierraleonetelegraph.com/sierra-leone-election-2018-noslinas-suna-nallo-and-kwame-fitzjohn-on-voa-tv-today/|title = Sierra Leone Election 2018 – Noslina's Suna Nallo and Kwame Fitzjohn on VOA TV Today|date = 7 March 2018}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
The [[President of Sierra Leone]] is elected using a modified [[two-round system]], with a candidate having to receive more than 55% of the vote in the first round to be elected.<ref>[http://www.thesierraleonetelegraph.com/sierra-leone-2018-elections-taking-parliament-and-losing-the-presidency/ Sierra Leone 2018 elections – taking parliament and losing the presidency] The Sierra Leone Telegraph, 11 December 2017</ref> If this is not achieved, a run-off will be held.

The 132 elected members of [[Parliament of Sierra Leone|Parliament]] (increased from 112 in the [[2012 Sierra Leonean general election|2012 elections]]) were elected from single-member constituencies by [[first-past-the-post voting]].<ref>[http://www.ifes.org/sites/default/files/2018_ifes_sierra_leone_general_elections_faqs_final.pdf Elections in Sierra Leone: 2018 General Elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200913015045/http://www.ifes.org/sites/default/files/2018_ifes_sierra_leone_general_elections_faqs_final.pdf |date=2020-09-13 }} IFES</ref><ref>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2281_B.htm Electoral system] IPU</ref> The remaining 14 seats are reserved for Paramount Chiefs, who are elected indirectly.<ref>[https://data.ipu.org/content/sierra-leone?chamber_id=13524 Sierra Leone Parliament] Inter-Parliamentary Union</ref>

==Presidential candidates==
A total of 16 candidates registered to contest the elections;<ref name=AN>[http://www.africanews.com/2018/02/21/sierra-leone-elects-new-president-the-top-six-contenders/ Sierra Leone elects new president: Profiles of top six contenders] Africa News, 21 February 2018</ref> 14 men and two women.<ref>[http://www.africanews.com/2018/03/06/2018-sierra-leone-general-elections-the-voting-process// 2018 Sierra Leone general elections: The voting process] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181210102844/http://www.africanews.com/2018/03/06/2018-sierra-leone-general-elections-the-voting-process/ |date=2018-12-10 }} Africa News, 6 March 2018</ref>

President Koroma personally selected foreign minister [[Samura Kamara]] as the [[All People's Congress]] candidate at the party's convention held on 15 November 2017 in the northern city of [[Makeni]]. The APC also selected deputy speaker of parliament [[Chernor Maju Bah]] as the party's vice presidential candidate.

The main opposition, the [[Sierra Leone People's Party]] (SLPP) selected former military Head of State, retired [[Brigadier general]] [[Julius Maada Bio]] as its presidential candidate at the party's national convention held on 15 November 2017 in [[Freetown]]. Bio's running mate was businessman Mohamed Juldeh Jalloh.

Former Vice President [[Samuel Sam-Sumana]] is the candidate of the Coalition for Change. His removal from office by President Koroma was challenged at the [[ECOWAS Court]], which in November 2017 ruled that the removal of Sumana was illegal.<ref>[https://www.bbc.com/pidgin/tori-42134874 ECOWAS Court: Sam-Sumana sack dey illegal] BBC Pidgin, 27 November 2017</ref>

Former United Nations senior official [[Kandeh Yumkella]] was chosen to be the presidential candidate of the National Grand Coalition (NGC), a breakaway faction of the SLPP. Yumkella's running mate is [[Mechanical engineering|mechanical engineer]] Andrew Keillie.

The newly formed Unity Party is fielding [[Femi Claudius Cole]], one of two female presidential candidates.<ref name="concord">{{cite news|last1=Koroma|first1=Dusuba|title=Lone Female Presidential Candidate Urges Issues-based Voting|url=http://slconcordtimes.com/lone-female-presidential-candidate-urges-issues-based-voting/|accessdate=26 November 2017|work=Sierra Leone Concord Times|date=8 November 2017|archive-date=24 February 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180224110822/http://slconcordtimes.com/lone-female-presidential-candidate-urges-issues-based-voting/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

Former Minister of Internal Affairs and Minister of Lands Musa Tarawally was nominated as the presidential candidate of the Citizens Democratic Party.<ref name=AN/>

