==Controversy==
Due to the fact that the election falls outside of the five-year term plus three months limit, constitutional lawyers have criticised the announcement.<ref name="thesierraleonetelegraph.com"/>

=== Dual citizenship ===
The previously dormant issue of dual citizenship was raised during the election. The ruling party, APC, raised the issue citing Section 76(1) of the 1991 Constitution, which states that ''"No person shall be qualified for election as a Member of Parliament — if he is a naturalised citizen of Sierra Leone or is a citizen of a country other than Sierra Leone having become such a citizen voluntarily or is under a declaration of allegiance to such a country."''<ref>{{cite book|title=The Constitution of Sierra Leone, 1991|url=http://necsl2018.org/the-constitution-of-sierra-leone-1991/}}</ref> The dual citizenship debate engulfed three presidential candidates: Kandeh Kolleh Yumkella of the National Grand Coalition (NGC), Samura Kamara of the All People's Congress (APC), and Mohamed Kamarainba Mansaray of the Alliance Democratic Party (ADP). Yumkella claimed he denounced his American citizenship in 2017, while Mansaray said he has never held any other citizenship.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Dauphine|first1=Edna Browne|title=Kamarainba denies dual citizenship|url=https://awoko.org/2018/01/18/sierra-leone-news-kamarainba-denies-dual-citizenship-discloses-income/|website=Awoko|accessdate=14 March 2018}}</ref> Two cases were brought to the Supreme Court: one filed on 5 February by an activist of the ruling All Peoples Congress party claiming that Yumkella is a ''naturalised citizen'' and therefore unqualified to contest the elections; and another filed by Charles Margai, a flag bearer for People's Movement for Democratic Change (PMDC), claiming that the APC’s standard bearer is unqualified to run for president because he is a dual citizen and did not resign his ministerial position – for which he was paid from the state’s consolidated revenue – long enough to meet the constitutional requirement.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Thomas|first1=Abdul Rashid|title=Yumkella at the Supreme Court|url=http://www.thesierraleonetelegraph.com/kandeh-yumkella-at-the-supreme-court-today-sierra-leones-stability-at-risk/|website=The Sierra Leone Telegraph|accessdate=14 March 2018|date=28 February 2018}}</ref> The defense of Yumkella appealed to the Supreme Court to have two of the judges removed from the case, then the matter was adjourned until 28 March (after the 7 March election) for the two new judges to study the case.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Bangura|first1=Bernice|title=Sierra Leone Supreme Court Makes Decision On Kandeh Yumkella|url=http://sierraloaded.net/breaking-sierra-leone-supreme-court-makes-decision-kandeh-yumkella/|website=Sierraloaded|accessdate=14 March 2018|date=6 March 2018}}</ref> The dual citizenship issue will be remembered by citizens for propagating the slang term ''two-SIM'' to describe a person with dual citizenship.

==Results==
The National Electoral Commission reported 3,178,663 registered voters (of which 1,654,228 were female) and a voter turnout of 2,676,549 (84.2%) across 3,300 polling centres and 11,122 polling stations.<ref name=ByNumbers>{{cite web|title=7 March 2018 Elections in Sierra Leone by Numbers|url=http://necsl2018.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/Election-Figures_1403.pdf|website=National Electoral Commission|accessdate=14 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180315070525/http://necsl2018.org/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/Election-Figures_1403.pdf|archive-date=15 March 2018|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref>

